<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Extend a Pattern: Mini-Lesson Review</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00830</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Extend a Pattern: Mini-Lesson Review</md:title>
    <md:uuid>fa31e04f-a2db-4ebf-ab17-4634895b5a2e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <note class="mini-lesson-question" id="sect-00001">
      <title>Question #3: Extend a Pattern</title>
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
        <para id="para-00001"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_OR_U4_Q3"/></para>
        </problem>
        </exercise>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Use a Rule to Extend a Pattern</title>
      <para id="para-00003">Pattern rules can use one or more mathematical operations to describe
        the relationship between consecutive numbers in a list of numbers, or sequence. Knowing the
        pattern rule will help you extend the pattern. To extend the pattern means to use the
        pattern rule to write the numbers that would come next in the sequence.</para>
      <para id="para-00004">Try to see the difference between consecutive numbers. It will help you
        understand the relationship between the numbers.</para>
      <para id="para-00005">Find the next two numbers in the following sequence:</para>
      <para id="para-00006">3, 6, 9, 12, . . .</para>
      <para id="para-00007">First, figure out the pattern rule. The numbers are increasing, so the
        rule likely involves addition or multiplication.</para>
      <list id="list-00001">
        <item>Look at the first two terms, 3 and 6. What operation could you perform on 3 to get 6? <list id="list-00002">
            <item>You could add 3. </item>
            <item>You could multiply by 2. </item>
            <item>You could do a combination of two or more operations. </item>
          </list>
        </item>

        <item>Look at the second two terms, 6 and 9. <list id="list-00003">
            <item>If you add 3 to 6, you get 9. So the pattern rule “add 3” seems to work. </item>
          </list>
        </item>

        <item>Make sure the pattern rule “add 3” works for every term. <list id="list-00004">
            <item><m:math>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>9</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>12</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math></item>
          </list>
        </item>

      </list>
      <para id="para-00008">The pattern rule works. To extend the pattern, apply the pattern rule of
        “add 3” to the last term, 12, to get the next term, and then “add 3” to the result to get
        another term. The answer is that the extended pattern is:</para>
      <para id="para-00009">3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, . . .</para>
      <para id="para-00010">You can find a particular term in the pattern by extending the pattern
        until you’ve reached the term you are looking for.</para>
      <para id="para-00011">What is the seventh number in the following sequence?</para>
      <para id="para-00012">64, 32, 16, 8, . . .</para>
      <para id="para-00013">First, figure out the pattern rule. The numbers are decreasing, so the
        rule likely involves subtraction or division.</para>
      <list id="list-00005">
        <item>Look at the first two terms, 64 and 32. What operation could you perform on 64 to get
          32? <list id="list-00006">
            <item>You could subtract 32. </item>
            <item>You could divide by 2. </item>
            <item>You could do a combination of two or more operations. </item>
          </list>
        </item>

        <item>Look at the second two terms, 32 and 16. <list id="list-00007">
            <item>If you subtract 32 from 32, you get 0, not 16. So the pattern rule “subtract 32”
          does not work. </item>
            <item>If you divide 32 by 2, you get 16. So the pattern rule “divide by 2” seems to
          work. </item>
          </list>
        </item>

        <item>Make sure the pattern rule “divide by 2” works for every term. <list id="list-00008">
            <item><m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>16</m:mn>
                  <m:mspace width="0.278em"/>
                  <m:mi>÷</m:mi>
                  <m:mspace width="0.278em"/>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mspace width="0.278em"/>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mspace width="0.278em"/>
                  <m:mn>8</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
            </item>
          </list>
        </item>

      </list>
      <para id="para-00014">The pattern rule works. To extend the pattern, apply the pattern rule of
        “divide by 2” to the last term, 8, to get the next term, and continue dividing by 2 until
        you reach the seventh term.</para>
      <para id="para-00015">64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1</para>
      <para id="para-00016">The seventh term of the sequence is 1.</para>
      <note class="try" id="note-00001">
        <title>Try It: Use a Rule to Extend a Pattern</title>
<para id="para-00017">Describe the rule
        and find the next two terms in the sequence.</para>
<para id="para-00018">3, 9, 27, 81, . . .</para>
<exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">

            <para id="para-00019">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis>
        button to compare your work. </para>

          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00020">Here’s how to identify a rule to extend a pattern:</para>

            <para id="para-00021">The numbers are increasing, so the rule likely involves addition
        or multiplication.</para>

            <list id="list-00009">
              <item>Look at the first two terms, 3 and 9. What operation could you perform on 3 to
        get 9? <list id="list-00010">
                  <item>You could add 6. </item>
                  <item>You could multiply by 3. </item>
                  <item>You could do a combination of two or more operations. </item>
                </list>
              </item>

              <item>Look at the second two terms, 9 and 27. <list id="list-00011">
                  <item>If you add 6 to 9, you get 15, not 27. So the pattern rule “add 6” does not
        work. </item>
                  <item>If you multiply 9 by 3, you get 27. So the rule “multiply by 3” seems to
        work. </item>
                </list>
              </item>

              <item>Make sure the pattern rule “multiply by 3” works for every term. <list id="list-00012">
                  <item>
                    <m:math display="inline">
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>27</m:mn>
                        <m:mspace width="0.278em"/>
                        <m:mi>×</m:mi>
                        <m:mspace width="0.278em"/>
                        <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                        <m:mspace width="0.278em"/>
                        <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                        <m:mspace width="0.278em"/>
                        <m:mn>81</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math></item>
                </list>
              </item>

            </list>

            <para id="para-00022">The pattern rule works. To extend the pattern, apply the pattern
        rule of “multiply by 3” to the last term, 81, to get the next term, and then “multiply by 3”
        to the result to get another term. The answer is that the extended pattern is:</para>

            <para id="para-00023">3, 9, 27, 81, 243, 729, . . .</para>

            <para id="para-00024">The rule is “multiply by 3,” and the next two terms are 243 and
        729.</para>

          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note></section>

      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00003">
        <title>Check Your Understanding</title>
  
        <exercise id="a3d095a4-fa45-4b3a-af9f-a0495c820309" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00025">Which describes the rule and the next two terms in the sequence?
              2, 6, 10, 14, …</para>
            <para id="para-00026">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <list list-type="enumerated" number-style="lower-alpha" id="list-00013">
            <item><para id="para-00027">Add 4; 18, 22</para></item>
            <item><para id="para-00028">Multiply by 3; 42, 126</para></item>
            <item><para id="para-00029">Multiply by 4; 56, 224</para></item>
            <item><para id="para-00030">Add 3; 17, 20</para></item>
          </list>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00031">That’s correct! Check yourself: Subtracting consecutive terms
              results in the difference 4. This means you add 4 to each term to get the next term.</para>
            <para id="para-00032"> Incorrect. Let’s try again. The result of subtracting consecutive
              terms is 4. This means you add 4 to get the next term, not multiply.</para>
              <para id="para-00033">Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The result of dividing the first two terms is 3. This might indicate a pattern of multiplying by 3, not adding 3. The answer is Add 4; 18, 22.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
    </section>
